example.org';\n```\nThere's no schema or DDL in the repo, so I couldn't check which indexes exist. The findings below assume none beyond the primary keys.\n\n## Scans the query forces\n\n1. **Full scan of `users` on `lower(u.email)`.** Wrapping the column in `lower()` makes a plain index on `email` unusable, because the planner can't match the expression to the index. Every user row is read and lowercased to find one match. This is the main problem.\n2. **Full scan of `orders` on `o.user_id`.** If `orders.user_id` has no index, the join has to scan all of `orders` (hash join) or probe it repeatedly (nested loop). Many databases don't index foreign keys automatically. Postgres doesn't, and InnoDB does.\n3. **Extra row width from `SELECT *`.** This pulls every column from both tables, including `u.id` and `o.user_id` twice. It stops index-only scans and sends more data to the client than needed. It also breaks when both tables have columns with the same name, such as `id` or `created_at`.\n\n## Fixes\n\n**1. Add an expression index (Postgres):**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n```\nThis turns scan 1 into an index lookup. In MySQL 8, use a functional index: `((lower(email)))`. SQL Server needs a computed column with an index.\n\n**Or avoid the function.** If you control the data, store emails lowercased and query `WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'` against a normal unique index. In Postgres you can use `citext` instead. A unique index on the normalized email also guarantees one user per address.\n\n**2. Index the join column:**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\nThis turns scan 2 into an index range lookup. If you usually sort by date, `(user_id, created_at)` covers that too.\n\n**3. Select only the columns you need:**\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\nAdjust the column list to what the caller uses.\n\n## Verify\nRun `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after. You should see `Seq Scan on users` become an `Index Scan` on `idx_users_email_lower`, and `orders` become an index scan on `idx_orders_user_id`. If you share the schema or the current `EXPLAIN` output, I can confirm which of these apply.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}